# DeepKids Mission Book: Plants, Core level (about age 8-9). Book-independent; works with any board.
# A book is a worksheet with "pages" (stations) instead of "blocks". See app/content/SPEC.md (Mission books, The DeepKids way).
STORY = {"type": "story", "title": "The Neem Who Was Made of Breath", "text": [
 "I am a neem tree, and I have stood at the corner of Kumbhar Lane for eighty-one years. I do not have eyes, but I know everyone who lives here. I know them by their breath.",
 "You think that is strange. Let me explain. Every time you breathe out, a little puff of invisible gas leaves your body. You call it carbon dioxide. You think it is nothing, just old air. But to me it is food. I pull it in through millions of tiny mouths on the undersides of my leaves, and with the sunlight that falls on me, and the water my roots drink from deep under the lane, I turn it into sugar, and the sugar into wood, and leaves, and the little bitter fruits the parrots love.",
 "So I am made of your breath. My trunk, my branches, every one of my leaves: they are built mostly out of the air. Out of the breath of everyone who ever sat beneath me.",
 "I remember Shanta when she was a baby, eighty years ago, sleeping in a cloth cradle tied to my lowest branch while her mother pounded rice. I breathed in her baby breath, and some of it became the ring of wood I grew that year. Shanta grew up, and grew old, and now she is the grandmother of the lane, and she still sits under me every evening. Her breath has changed. It is slower now. But I know it.",
 "And here is the other half of the secret, the half that you forget. When I make my food, I breathe out too. I breathe out the gas you call oxygen, and you breathe it in. So Shanta and I have been breathing each other for eighty years. Her out-breath is my in-breath. My out-breath is hers. We are not two separate things, the old woman and the tree. We are one long, slow breath, going back and forth.",
 "This summer, men came with measuring tapes. The road was to be widened, they said, for the trucks. The tree at the corner would have to go.",
 "\"What is it worth?\" asked the man with the clipboard. \"The timber, I mean. Neem wood is good wood. Five thousand rupees, perhaps.\"",
 "Shanta's granddaughter, Gauri, was nine. She had been listening. \"Five thousand rupees?\" she said. \"Then who will pay for the shade? In May it is ten degrees cooler under the neem than on the road. Who will pay for that?\"",
 "The man laughed. But Gauri kept going. She had a notebook. \"Who will pay the parrots, and the bulbuls, and the squirrels who live in it? Who will pay for the oxygen? Who will pay my Aaji for eighty years of shade? You can only put a price on the wood because the wood is the only part you can sell.\"",
 "The man stopped laughing.",
 "The next morning, when the men came back with their saws, they found the women of Kumbhar Lane standing around my trunk. Shanta, with her walking stick. Gauri's mother, who sells vegetables. The washerwoman, the potter's wife, the girls from the school. They held hands around me, the way the women of Reni village in the Himalayas once held hands around their trees, long ago, and would not let them be cut.",
 "\"This tree is made of our breath,\" Gauri told the men. \"My grandmother's breath, and my mother's, and mine. If you cut it, you cut that.\"",
 "I do not know if the men understood about the breath. Perhaps it was the women standing so quietly. Perhaps it was the shade, which was very pleasant that hot morning, and made them think. They went away. They made the road go round the corner instead of through it.",
 "That evening, Shanta sat under me as she always does. Gauri sat beside her. They were quiet, breathing, and I was quiet, breathing.",
 "Somewhere in me, a new ring of wood is growing this year. It is made of the air of that morning: the breath of the women who held hands, and the breath of the men who went away. I will keep it for a hundred years."],
 "epiphany": "Photosynthesis, felt: a tree is built mostly from carbon dioxide taken from the air (plus water), using sunlight; and it gives out the oxygen we breathe. So wood is made of air, and people and trees breathe each other: one cycle. The turn is Gauri's line, 'This tree is made of our breath.' Underneath: told from the tree's point of view; a price that counts only what can be sold; and the women who protect what belongs to everyone (Gaura Devi and the women of Reni, Chipko movement, 1974).",
 "ask": ["Where does the wood of a tree come from? Were you surprised?", "Why does the tree say it is 'made of your breath'?", "What do the old woman and the tree give each other?", "The man said the tree was worth ₹5,000. What did Gauri mean when she said he could only price the wood?", "Why do you think the women held hands around the tree?"]}

