  "This book goes far beyond naming parts. The big ideas: a plant builds itself mostly out of air; plants and animals breathe each other; plants sense and respond in ways scientists are still arguing about; 'weed' is a human word; and seeds were shared by farmers for 10,000 years. Let your child disagree with you and with the book. Ask 'How could we find out?' more than you give answers.",
  "The case has a real answer: the tulsi is suffering from three things at once. Too much water with no drainage hole (roots are drowning: they need air, and soggy soil starts to rot, hence the smell), too little light in the kitchen corner (leaves can't make enough food, so they yellow), and the move from balcony to kitchen. Don't give it away; the clues lead there.",
