  {"station": "Station 6", "title": "Are plants clever?", "mins": 40, "blocks": [
   {"type": "read", "title": "A plant that learns?", "text": [
     "Plants have no brain and no nerves. And yet they sense light, gravity, touch, water, and even the chemicals other plants give off. The touch-me-not (lajwanti, Mimosa pudica) folds its leaves in a second when touched.",
     "In 2014, a scientist named Monica Gagliano tested whether a plant could learn. She dropped potted touch-me-nots a short, harmless distance again and again. At first they folded their leaves every time. After a while, they stopped folding: as if they had learned the drop was nothing to fear. When she shook them in a new way, they folded again, so they weren't just tired. She said they remembered for weeks.",
     "Some scientists were excited: maybe plants can learn without a brain! Others said: careful. 'Learning' and 'intelligence' are words we made for animals with brains. Maybe something simpler is going on. The argument is still going on today."]},
   {"type": "investigate", "title": "Touch-me-not lab", "q": "Does a touch-me-not get used to being touched?",
    "need": ["a touch-me-not plant", "a stopwatch", "a soft paintbrush or feather"],
    "steps": ["Touch one leaf gently with the brush. Time how long until it opens again.", "Choose another leaf. Touch it once every minute for 10 minutes. Does it fold less as time goes on?", "Now touch a leaf that hasn't been touched today. What happens?", "Be gentle: the plant spends energy each time it folds."],
    "table": {"cols": ["Touch", "Did it fold? (fully / a bit / no)", "Time to open again"], "rows": [["1", None, None], ["3", None, None], ["5", None, None], ["8", None, None], ["10", None, None]]},
    "conclude": ["What I noticed: ", "I think the plant is (learning / tired / something else) because "],
    "ans": "Results vary. A leaf touched many times may fold less or reopen faster. Whether that is 'learning' or tiredness is exactly what scientists argue about."},
   {"type": "debate", "title": "The big argument", "q": "Can a plant be intelligent?",
    "sides": [{"who": "A scientist who says yes, in its own way", "says": ["Plants sense their world, make choices like which way to grow roots, and change what they do based on what happened before. That's a kind of intelligence, just not ours.", "We call only brains clever because we have one."]},
              {"who": "A scientist who says no", "says": ["A thermostat also senses and responds. We don't call it intelligent.", "Using words like 'learning' and 'feeling' for plants can mislead people. We should describe what they do without guessing what it's like inside."]}]}]},
